## Preliminaries: start a session

The same query and loading API works with `:port` and `:nif`. Choose a backend
here and close the session in the final cell before starting the workflow again.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
backend = :port
{:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)
```

## State the problem in Prolog

`Rows` has one element per column. Its value is that column's queen row, so
`[2,4,1,3]` means queens at column/row pairs `(1,2)`, `(2,4)`, `(3,1)`, `(4,3)`.
The constraints eliminate attacks; `labeling/2` searches for complete placements.

```elixir
search_program = ~S"""
:- module(search, [queens/2]).
:- use_module(library(clpfd)).
queens(N, Rows) :-
    length(Rows, N), Rows ins 1..N,
    safe(Rows), labeling([ff], Rows).
safe([]).
safe([Q|Qs]) :- diagonals(Qs, Q, 1), safe(Qs).
diagonals([], _, _).
diagonals([Q|Qs], Q0, D) :-
    Q0 #\= Q, abs(Q0-Q) #\= D, D1 #= D+1, diagonals(Qs, Q0, D1).
"""
```

The complete source is inline. `load/3` expects a file, so write it temporarily
and remove it after SWI has loaded it. Reusing the filename makes reevaluation
a reconsult of the same module.

```elixir
path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "swi-livebook-search-#{System.pid()}.pl")
File.write!(path, search_program)

try do
  :ok = SWI.load(session, path)
after
  File.rm!(path)
end
```

## Exhaust a finite search

Four queens has two solutions in this representation. We collect the entire
finite search with `Enum.to_list/1` and inspect the returned integer lists.

```elixir
four_queens = SWI.query(session, "search:queens(4, Rows)") |> Enum.map(& &1["Rows"])
assert Enum.sort(four_queens) == [[2, 4, 1, 3], [3, 1, 4, 2]]

Kino.DataTable.new(
  for {rows, index} <- Enum.with_index(four_queens, 1),
      do: %{solution: index, rows: inspect(rows)}
)
```

To display a board, put a queen at the returned row for each column. The
renderer contains no solving logic.

```elixir
board = fn placement ->
  rows =
    for row <- 1..length(placement) do
      cells =
        for {queen, column} <- Enum.with_index(placement, 1),
            do: {"column #{column}", if(queen == row, do: "♛", else: "·")}

      Map.new([{"row", row} | cells])
    end

  keys = ["row" | Enum.map(1..length(placement), &"column #{&1}")]
  Kino.DataTable.new(rows, keys: keys, sorting_enabled: false)
end

board.(hd(four_queens))
```

### Try it yourself

Ask for `search:queens(3, Rows)` in a new cell and collect its answers. Predict
whether an empty list means a failed operation or a completed search.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Three queens has no nonattacking placement. The stream finishes with zero
answers; that is a successful search with no solutions, rather than an error.

</details>

## Stop after three solutions

For eight queens, consume only three placements. The stream preserves SWI's
choice points between pulls and closes the remaining search on early halt.
The assertions check the mathematical constraints independently of the solver.

```elixir
eight_queens =
  SWI.query(session, "search:queens(8, Rows)")
  |> Enum.take(3)
  |> Enum.map(& &1["Rows"])

assert length(Enum.uniq(eight_queens)) == 3

for placement <- eight_queens do
  assert Enum.sort(placement) == Enum.to_list(1..8)

  for {row, column} <- Enum.with_index(placement),
      {other, index} <- Enum.with_index(placement),
      column != index do
    assert abs(row - other) != abs(column - index)
  end
end

{:ok, true} = SWI.call(session, "true")
board.(hd(eight_queens))
```

A session permits one active stream at a time. Use separate sessions for
concurrent searches. If you suspend an enumerable manually, halt its
continuation explicitly when finished with it.

## Consume a finite prefix of an infinite relation

An unbounded Prolog generator can be useful when Elixir asks for a finite
prefix. Collecting the entire generator with `Enum.to_list/1` would never finish.

```elixir
first_ten = SWI.query(session, "between(1,inf,N)") |> Enum.take(10)
assert Enum.map(first_ten, & &1["N"]) == Enum.to_list(1..10)
Kino.DataTable.new(first_ten)
```

## Close the session

```elixir
:ok = SWI.stop(session)
```

